```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: Data platform and ML teams that store tabular numeric features as float16 in Parquet (to halve storage/bandwidth costs) and subsequently compute summary statistics (mean/std) using pandas Series.mean()/std() during EDA, validation, or feature monitoring. Triggered at production-scale row counts where N × magnitude exceeds the fp16 accumulator limit of 65504 (e.g., California housing N=20640, MedInc mean=3.87 and Latitude 35.6 both produce pandas inf).
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Real win, not microbenchmark-only. The real-data arm on California housing (N=20640, native unmodified magnitudes) demonstrates 8/9 features yield pandas inf/nan after fp16 cast, including small-mean features (MedInc 3.87, Latitude 35.6); 6/9 exhibit the full 3-way divergence (pandas inf / numpy ~1e-4 relerr / sklearn ~1e-6). The failure mode occurs at realistic row counts and magnitudes, not synthetic extremes. However, the "win" is a bug/negative result (silent incorrect statistics), not a performance or capability improvement.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [fp16 tabular feature storage is uncommon in practice—most feature stores and pipelines use float32/float64 for numeric features; fp16 is typically reserved for embeddings/model weights where precision loss is expected and tooling handles it; pandas users encountering inf/nan observe a visible failure (not completely silent) and will investigate; trivial workaround exists (astype to float32/float64 before reduction, or use numpy/sklearn which upcast), reducing urgency to adopt a fix; no evidence provided that production systems depend on pandas fp16 reductions being correct, or that fp16 feature storage is prevalent enough to make this a widespread operational risk]
RATIONALE: Packet L0 RESULT (real-data arm): "8/9 features -> pandas inf/nan after fp16 INCLUDING MedInc (mean=3.87) + Latitude (35.6) -- a SMALL MEAN does NOT protect (the running fp16 SUM blows past 65504 at moderate N); 6/9 show the FULL 3-way divergence (pandas inf / numpy ~1e-4 relerr / sklearn ~1e-6); only AveBedrms (tiny values) escaped. Native magnitudes, not rigged." Control (fp32/fp64, 42 cells) confirms fp16 ISOLATED as cause with all tools agreeing. Prior-art section notes "no framework documents the cross-tool divergence (pandas to_numeric/numpy reduction notes mention generic precision loss, neither warns a STORED fp16 feature makes .mean() return inf vs quantized by tool)." The coupling is verified in source (pandas/core/nanops.py L697-712: dtype.kind=="f" => dtype_sum=dtype AND dtype_count=dtype, no upcast). However, packet stress-test B explicitly flags: "do people ACTUALLY store fp16 feature columns AND reduce them with pandas .mean()/.std() by default? If fp16 feature storage is rare, or everyone normalizes first, the seam is real-but-rarely-triggered." No prevalence data is provided for fp16 tabular storage in production feature stores. The actionable guidance collapses to known folklore cited in packet (bf16/fp16 FMA-accum precision 1904.06376, Kahan summation: "use float64 for reductions"), not a new operational practice. The divergence is real and reproducible, but the deployment regime is narrow; operational impact is limited to teams that have already chosen an uncommon storage format (fp16 for tabular features) and use pandas (not numpy/sklearn) for reductions without upcasting.
```
